<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SECURITY: deny cleartext HTTP for the entire app. The only plain-HTTP use in
  the codebase is the geolocation probe (NetProbe), which deliberately uses raw
  sockets (exempt from this policy by design) and carries no user data — see
  docs/SECURITY_AUDIT.md. This config is defense-in-depth: it guarantees that
  no third-party library or future HttpURLConnection/OkHttp code can silently
  send cleartext traffic.

  TRUST ANCHORS: SYSTEM ONLY (audit 1.2.9-r3, mitigation for F-4).

  The 1.2.9 audit's only MitM finding was that trust is the platform CA store,
  so a device carrying an attacker-installed root CA can intercept the app's own
  TLS — the AI conversation with Google, the geolocation probe, the Psiphon
  front's reachability check. Certificate pinning was rejected then, and is
  rejected again, for a good reason: pinning
  `generativelanguage.googleapis.com` in a client that ships from a GitHub
  release and cannot be hot-fixed turns a routine Google certificate rotation
  into a permanently broken feature.

  This is the fix that removes the attack without that fragility. Android's
  default trust store on API 24+ already excludes user-added CAs for apps that
  target it, but the default is INHERITED, which means a future
  `targetSdk`/`base-config` edit could quietly restore it. Declaring the anchor
  set explicitly makes the property EXPLICIT and permanent:

    * `src="system"` — only the OS-shipped CAs are trusted.
    * `src="user"` is deliberately absent, so a root certificate installed
      through Settings (the standard way an MitM proxy such as mitmproxy,
      Burp or a hostile network's "install this certificate" page works)
      CANNOT validate a chain for this app. Interception of the app's own
      requests fails the handshake instead of succeeding silently.

  What this does NOT affect: the tunnel itself. WireGuard and MASQUE/QUIC carry
  their own key material and their own validation inside the engine, and the
  user's browsing traffic is not TLS-terminated here at all — it is forwarded.
  So no user-visible capability is lost, and debugging with a proxy simply needs
  a debuggable build (`debug-overrides` is not shipped, on purpose).
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
